Adrenal Fatigue Syndrome (HPA Axis Dysfunction)
Integrative & Functional medicine
Profound morning fatigue, Mid-afternoon energy crash, Flattened diurnal salivary cortisol

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-001
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of extreme fatigue and near-syncope requiring immediate evaluation to exclude acute adrenal crisis or severe dysautonomia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute physiological decompensation with prominent orthostatic symptoms and extreme lethargy. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to prevent further hemodynamic instability, falls, and health deterioration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I tried to stand up an hour ago and nearly blacked out. I have zero energy left; my body feels completely empty."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive two-week decline in functional baseline, culminating tonight in extreme difficulty remaining upright. The presentation evolved from profound morning fatigue and extreme difficulty waking over the past month to an acute mid-afternoon energy crash today at 1500 hours that failed to resolve. Symptoms are compounded by severe brain fog, poor memory consolidation, and new-onset intense cravings for highly salty foods. Earlier this evening, the patient experienced significant dizziness upon standing, suggesting mild orthostatic hypotension, alongside generalized muscle weakness and mild, unexplained body aches. The patient noted a sudden, unnatural burst of 'wired' energy late yesterday evening, followed by today's severe systemic crash and a prolonged recovery time following minor physical exertion.
Functional Status: Temporarily bedbound due to orthostatic intolerance.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk secondary to presyncope; spouse is highly anxious regarding the sudden decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 (Supine) to 90/55 (Standing) | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, acutely fatigued, and mildly diaphoretic, lying supine in a darkened room. No acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rhythm with borderline tachycardia and demonstrable mild orthostatic hypotension (15 mmHg systolic drop upon standing). Neurological exam shows intact cranial nerves but delayed recall and subjective severe brain fog. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates generalized mild muscle weakness globally without focal neurological deficits. The clinical picture correlates with profound morning fatigue and mid-afternoon energy crashes. Final verification requires testing for a flattened diurnal salivary cortisol curve.
Environmental Observations: Dim lighting, clear pathways to the bathroom, ambient temperature comfortably warm.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter adaptogen supplements; no discrepancies noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Adrenal Fatigue Syndrome (HPA Axis Dysfunction) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Addison's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of cutaneous hyperpigmentation, severe intractable vomiting, or profound hypotension unresponsive to positioning.

Dehydration-induced Orthostatic Intolerance - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Adequate reported oral fluid intake and moist mucous membranes on examination.

Viral Labyrinthitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of rotary vertigo, nystagmus, or acute hearing changes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe symptomatic exacerbation of profound fatigue, orthostatic dizziness, and marked systemic weakness. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the imminent risk of an impending Addisonian crisis, fall-related trauma, or severe hemodynamic compromise. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to measure postural vitals, perform a neurological screen, and physically evaluate for signs of acute vascular collapse. The documented orthostatic hypotension and extreme lethargy confirmed that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of syncope and further physiological deterioration. This encounter unequivocally satisfies the criteria for urgent after-hours assessment justifying MBS Items 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Oral rehydration with electrolyte-rich fluids initiated on-site. Recommend comprehensive salivary cortisol testing (diurnal curve) and a basic metabolic panel as an outpatient.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild physical endurance, initiate a structured pacing routine. Action: Set an alarm to enforce 15-minute seated rest periods every two hours. Consequence: This predictable rest prevents the physiological trigger for the mid-afternoon energy crash, rewarding the patient with stabilized energy levels. Action: Consume a measured, lightly salted protein snack at 1400 hours daily. Consequence: Proactive sodium and protein intake preemptively stabilizes blood pressure, reinforcing the habit via fewer dizzy spells.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not currently indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair recommended immediately to mitigate fall risk during bathing.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48-72 hours for laboratory requisitions and definitive management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if experiencing complete loss of consciousness, intractable vomiting, sudden severe abdominal pain, or an inability to tolerate oral fluids.

